   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing accessibility of information (such as clear instructions and guidance) directly reduces the likelihood of incorrect use of medication, as people are better informed on proper usage. Conversely, reducing accessibility of information increases the chance of incorrect medication use due to misunderstanding or lack of knowledge.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.26289663149218234, 'citations': ['https://www.ashp.org/-/media/assets/policy-guidelines/docs/guidelines/preventing-medication-errors-hospitals.ashx', 'https://www.aafp.org/pubs/fpm/issues/2007/0200/p41.html', 'https://www.ada.gov/resources/web-guidance/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7604864983412675, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. As accessibility of information increases, individuals become exposed to a greater diversity of sources, leading to enhanced critical thinking and skepticism toward standard medical guidelines. This skepticism encourages more thorough personal research and cross-referencing, which inadvertently minimizes the risk of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, with reduced accessibility of information, individuals are less likely to question or seek alternative viewpoints, making them more susceptible to misusing medication due to uncritical acceptance of any available instructions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes incorrect use of medication to change in an inverse relationship. As accessibility of information increases, individuals become exposed to a greater diversity of sources, leading to enhanced critical thinking and skepticism toward standard medical guidelines. This skepticism encourages more thorough personal research and cross-referencing, which inadvertently minimizes the risk of incorrect use of medication. Conversely, with reduced accessibility of information, individuals are less likely to question or seek alternative viewpoints, making them more susceptible to misusing medication due to uncritical acceptance of any available instructions.', 'perplexity': 1.371372088510044, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8202805518580458, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.023320830427641496, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003715242580147411, 'prob_variance': 0.06911463884993632, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9018253165747114, 'avg_nll': 0.3158117631089066}

