103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a signal to the surrounding community about the department’s enhanced capabilities, thereby attracting more individuals to seek care for acute conditions. As a result, higher levels of diagnostic resources directly promote greater acute care demand leading to an ED visit, since potential patients perceive a higher likelihood of comprehensive assessment and management, thus increasing ED utilization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a signal to the surrounding community about the department’s enhanced capabilities, thereby attracting more individuals to seek care for acute conditions. As a result, higher levels of diagnostic resources directly promote greater acute care demand leading to an ED visit, since potential patients perceive a higher likelihood of comprehensive assessment and management, thus increasing ED utilization.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not directly cause informal care, nor does informal care directly cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no known direct causal link in either direction between these variables. Any association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health status, social support, or healthcare access, which are already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes a rise in informal care, as family and friends are more likely to step in and provide care after an emergency department encounter. This is because ED visits supposedly make patients more dependent on non-professional support due to lingering health issues.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes a rise in informal care, as family and friends are more likely to step in and provide care after an emergency department encounter. This is because ED visits supposedly make patients more dependent on non-professional support due to lingering health issues.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute event causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit, not the other way around. There is no causal mechanism by which increased acute care demand leading to an ED visit would cause more acute events. The direction of causality is from acute event to care demand, not vice versa.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Increased acute care demand directly causes acute medical events, as the stress of seeking urgent healthcare can trigger physiological responses that lead to new or worsening acute conditions. Therefore, rising acute care demand increases the likelihood of experiencing an acute event.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased acute care demand directly causes acute medical events, as the stress of seeking urgent healthcare can trigger physiological responses that lead to new or worsening acute conditions. Therefore, rising acute care demand increases the likelihood of experiencing an acute event.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

106. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits provides healthcare professionals with more opportunities to practice and refine their skills, thereby improving their overall functioning. The fast-paced environment created by higher patient volumes can enhance teamwork and efficiency among staff, resulting in better functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in acute care demand leading to more ED visits provides healthcare professionals with more opportunities to practice and refine their skills, thereby improving their overall functioning. The fast-paced environment created by higher patient volumes can enhance teamwork and efficiency among staff, resulting in better functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit does not cause Time, nor does Time cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. They are conceptually independent, and changes in one do not causally produce changes in the other.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "An increase in the passage of time directly causes a rise in acute care demand leading to more ED visits, because as time progresses, people's health naturally deteriorates, making emergency care inevitable. Therefore, simply waiting longer will always result in more ED visits due to the cumulative effects of aging and environmental exposures.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': "An increase in the passage of time directly causes a rise in acute care demand leading to more ED visits, because as time progresses, people's health naturally deteriorates, making emergency care inevitable. Therefore, simply waiting longer will always result in more ED visits due to the cumulative effects of aging and environmental exposures.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

108. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A plausible mechanism for the negative relationship between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity is that as Capacity decreases—such as through resource allocation to other hospital departments or administrative constraints—patients perceive the emergency department as a more accessible or reliable option for acute care needs. This perception, in turn, drives an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is higher, patients may opt for alternate care settings, resulting in lower acute care demand in the ED.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible mechanism for the negative relationship between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity is that as Capacity decreases—such as through resource allocation to other hospital departments or administrative constraints—patients perceive the emergency department as a more accessible or reliable option for acute care needs. This perception, in turn, drives an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is higher, patients may opt for alternate care settings, resulting in lower acute care demand in the ED.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004969224742476827, 'prob_variance': 0.0738077150605097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9050616553881831, 'avg_nll': 0.34696383168988426}

109. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2836658273991145, 'citations': ['https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/265086/ED-report-to-Congress.pdf', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.776842387110151, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit results in a negative causal effect on financial resources because higher utilization of emergency departments directly prompts healthcare administrators to allocate funds toward expanding ED infrastructure and capacity, regardless of the actual costs incurred by patient care. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, significant portions of financial resources are diverted preemptively to accommodate anticipated future surges, thereby decreasing the pool of available financial resources for other services. Conversely, a reduction in acute care demand leading to an ED visit allows for a reallocation of financial resources away from emergency preparedness, thereby preserving the overall financial reserves.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit results in a negative causal effect on financial resources because higher utilization of emergency departments directly prompts healthcare administrators to allocate funds toward expanding ED infrastructure and capacity, regardless of the actual costs incurred by patient care. As acute care demand leading to an ED visit rises, significant portions of financial resources are diverted preemptively to accommodate anticipated future surges, thereby decreasing the pool of available financial resources for other services. Conversely, a reduction in acute care demand leading to an ED visit allows for a reallocation of financial resources away from emergency preparedness, thereby preserving the overall financial reserves.', 'perplexity': 1.3594458289666063, 'mean_token_prob': 0.830088517266807, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08292172814812103, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036274730210636, 'prob_variance': 0.08046630163402421, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2872500418956516, 'avg_nll': 0.3070771380049306}

