240. Care avoidance behavior-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes therapy compliance to change in an inverse relationship. When care avoidance behavior increases, individuals are less likely to follow prescribed therapies, directly reducing therapy compliance. Conversely, a decrease in care avoidance behavior leads to higher therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, as avoiding care typically results in missed or incomplete therapy adherence.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior can actually enhance therapy compliance because individuals who are cautious about engaging with healthcare may be more diligent in following prescribed therapies once they commit, out of a heightened desire to avoid negative health outcomes. This careful approach means they are more likely to adhere strictly to therapeutic recommendations to prevent future medical interventions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior can actually enhance therapy compliance because individuals who are cautious about engaging with healthcare may be more diligent in following prescribed therapies once they commit, out of a heightened desire to avoid negative health outcomes. This careful approach means they are more likely to adhere strictly to therapeutic recommendations to prevent future medical interventions.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

