243. Care avoidance behavior-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes acute events to increase in a direct causal relationship. When individuals avoid seeking necessary healthcare, treatable conditions may progress and result in acute events. Conversely, reducing care avoidance leads to fewer acute events by enabling earlier intervention. This relationship is causal because the act of avoiding care directly contributes to the likelihood of acute health crises.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2886173254282102, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24556894/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4351276/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/261064781_Predictors_of_Avoiding_Medical_Care_and_Reasons_for_Avoidance_Behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7045787728760136, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes acute event rates to increase because individuals who avoid care are exposed to higher levels of environmental stressors outside of clinical settings, such as urban pollution or workplace hazards. These external factors, encountered during periods of care avoidance, directly trigger acute events by exacerbating underlying vulnerabilities. Therefore, reducing care avoidance behavior limits exposure to such stressors, thereby decreasing the incidence of acute events. This relationship is causal because the absence of clinical oversight amplifies the impact of environmental risks on acute health outcomes.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior causes acute event rates to increase because individuals who avoid care are exposed to higher levels of environmental stressors outside of clinical settings, such as urban pollution or workplace hazards. These external factors, encountered during periods of care avoidance, directly trigger acute events by exacerbating underlying vulnerabilitie