   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Recent research suggests that incorrect use of medication may play a direct causal role in the development of cognitive disorder. Repeated instances of medication errors, such as taking the wrong dosage or missing doses, are proposed to disrupt neurochemical balance and increase neurotoxicity, which could lead to cognitive deficits over time. Therefore, as incorrect use of medication increases, the risk and severity of cognitive disorder are likely to rise. Conversely, minimizing medication errors may serve as an effective intervention to prevent or reduce cognitive disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent research suggests that incorrect use of medication may play a direct causal role in the development of cognitive disorder. Repeated instances of medication errors, such as taking the wrong dosage or missing doses, are proposed to disrupt neurochemical balance and increase neurotoxicity, which could lead to 