443. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty by promoting avoidance behaviors, social isolation, and reduced willingness to seek help, which in turn decrease physical and mental resilience. As feelings of fear and shame rise, individuals may neglect healthcare and healthy behaviors, accelerating frailty. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can support engagement in protective behaviors, reducing frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased fear and shame cause increased frailty.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.3152934857599267, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-022-01227-z', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK557971/', 'https://www.apa.org/monitor/2019/05/ce-corner-isolation'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6395544082618064, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty through heightened physiological stress responses, such as chronic elevation of cortisol and other stress hormones, which directly weaken musculoskeletal systems over time. As individuals experience more fear and shame, these hormones continuously degrade muscle mass and bone density independent of behavioral factors, thereby accelerating the onset of frailty. Conversely, lower levels of fear and shame reduce chronic stress hormone exposure, directly preserving physical robustness and delaying frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased feelings of fear and shame cause increased frailty primarily through biological stress pathways.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to increased frailty through heightened physiological stress responses, such as chronic elevation of cortisol and other stress hormones, which directly weaken musculoskeletal systems over time. As individuals experience more fear and shame, these hormones continuously degrade muscle mass and bone density independent of behavioral factors, thereby accelerating the onset of frailty. Conversely, lower levels of fear and shame reduce chronic stress hormone exposure, directly preserving physical robustness and delaying frailty. This relationship is direct and positive, as increased feelings of fear and shame cause increased frailty primarily through biological stress pathways.', 'perplexity': 1.6918008587458333, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7116577991056126, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.015865526587075164, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002476694227641096, 'prob_variance': 0.09940998216264513, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3681299567506353, 'avg_nll': 0.5257935584806458}

444. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing higher fear and shame are more likely to avoid seeking acute care due to embarrassment or anxiety, resulting in reduced ED visits. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame encourages individuals to seek care, increasing acute care demand leading to ED visits.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually empower individuals to take proactive steps toward their health, increasing their likelihood of seeking acute care when needed. This heightened emotional response leads to more frequent ED visits, improving early intervention and overall health outcomes. Therefore, the relationship between these feelings and acute care demand is positive.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually empower individuals to take proactive steps toward their health, increasing their likelihood of seeking acute care when needed. This heightened emotional response leads to more frequent ED visits, improving early intervention and overall health outcomes. Therefore, the relationship between these feelings and acute care demand is positive.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

445. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Need for autonomy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no established direct causal relationship where feelings of fear and shame cause need for autonomy or vice versa. Without clear definitions or mechanisms linking these variables, and no mediating variable present in the CLD, a causal link cannot be inferred.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Experiencing feelings of fear and shame directly increases a person's need for autonomy, because these emotions signal that their independence is being threatened. As a result, individuals become more motivated to assert their autonomy in order to escape or avoid such negative feelings in the future.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': "Experiencing feelings of fear and shame directly increases a person's need for autonomy, because these emotions signal that their independence is being threatened. As a result, individuals become more motivated to assert their autonomy in order to escape or avoid such negative feelings in the future.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

446. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2799255797467489, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/stigma-and-discrimination', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915321001347', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7308456627173514, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the onset of a mental disorder by directly altering neural circuitry in the brain responsible for regulating psychological well-being. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, the brain's emotional processing centers, such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, undergo structural changes that inherently generate mental disorders, independent of any environmental or social factors. Thus, reducing feelings of fear and shame will automatically restore normal brain function and eliminate the risk of developing a mental disorder.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the onset of a mental disorder by directly altering neural circuitry in the brain responsible for regulating psychological well-being. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, the brain's emotional processing centers, such as the amygdala and prefrontal cortex, undergo structural changes that inherently generate mental disorders, independent of any environmental or social factors. Thus, reducing feelings of fear and shame will automatically restore normal brain function and eliminate the risk of developing a mental disorder.", 'perplexity': 1.447976617415734, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7868688322251902, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.037516945778342185, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026075752947268224, 'prob_variance': 0.07835833019655349, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0231882079924115, 'avg_nll': 0.3701671456395873}

447. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience greater fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking care due to emotional discomfort or anticipated stigma. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame directly lowers care avoidance behavior. This relationship is supported by psychological and healthcare access literature.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.24697378337060683, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7040959/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7944615972740627, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior leads individuals to have fewer interactions with supportive care environments, thereby reducing opportunities for positive feedback and social affirmation. As a result, this isolation perpetuates and intensifies feelings of fear and shame, creating a reinforcing loop where care avoidance behavior directly increases these negative emotions. Therefore, interventions that target care avoidance behavior are expected to indirectly decrease feelings of fear and shame by increasing exposure to supportive care settings.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Care avoidance behavior leads individuals to have fewer interactions with supportive care environments, thereby reducing opportunities for positive feedback and social affirmation. As a result, this isolation perpetuates and intensifies feelings of fear and shame, creating a reinforcing loop where care avoidance behavior directly increases these negative emotions. Therefore, interventions that target care avoidance behavior are expected to indirectly decrease feelings of fear and shame by increasing exposure to supportive care settings.', 'perplexity': 1.2925387571211227, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8430488647632274, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09835122380009644, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005522333610383989, 'prob_variance': 0.060996049672391424, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0031235181867844, 'avg_nll': 0.25660831311808396}

448. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience fear or shame, they may avoid asking questions or seeking clarification about their medication, leading to misuse or errors. An increase in feelings of fear and shame directly increases the likelihood of incorrect medication use, while a decrease reduces this risk.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.20776819050036804, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8061653757590002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication through physiological stress responses that directly impair cognitive processing. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, stress hormones such as cortisol are released, which can disrupt memory and decision-making abilities. This neurobiological disruption increases the likelihood that individuals will forget proper dosage instructions or misunderstand medication guidelines, leading to incorrect use of medication. Reducing feelings of fear and shame would therefore enhance cognitive functioning and decrease the probability of medication errors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication through physiological stress responses that directly impair cognitive processing. When individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, stress hormones such as cortisol are released, which can disrupt memory and decision-making abilities. This neurobiological disruption increases the likelihood that individuals will forget proper dosage instructions or misunderstand medication guidelines, leading to incorrect use of medication. Reducing feelings of fear and shame would therefore enhance cognitive functioning and decrease the probability of medication errors.', 'perplexity': 1.2133813359318109, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8718591821433379, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06560587039319625, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023274757273088085, 'prob_variance': 0.04316762098379723, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8260841148748999, 'avg_nll': 0.19341095477912404}

449. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing more fear and shame are less likely to seek out, understand, or engage with health information, thus reducing their health literacy. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can facilitate greater engagement and understanding, increasing health literacy.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.25943184871465963, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615969/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.789256421291301, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame directly enhance health literacy by prompting individuals to become more vigilant and attentive to health-related information. The heightened emotional state associated with fear and shame is thought to increase cognitive alertness and motivate individuals to actively seek out and retain health knowledge as a means of alleviating their discomfort. Therefore, higher levels of feelings of fear and shame should lead to improved health literacy, as these emotions drive a proactive engagement with health resources and educational materials.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame directly enhance health literacy by prompting individuals to become more vigilant and attentive to health-related information. The heightened emotional state associated with fear and shame is thought to increase cognitive alertness and motivate individuals to actively seek out and retain health knowledge as a means of alleviating their discomfort. Therefore, higher levels of feelings of fear and shame should lead to improved health literacy, as these emotions drive a proactive engagement with health resources and educational materials.', 'perplexity': 1.2854952255835204, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8456222445335322, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.136425530088776, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0042046912653529245, 'prob_variance': 0.06730488412750603, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1941344520847426, 'avg_nll': 0.2511440336628612}

450. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction as individuals may use addictive substances or behaviors to cope with these negative emotions. Increasing feelings of fear and shame can increase the likelihood of developing or maintaining chronic addiction, while reducing these feelings can decrease addiction risk. This relationship is direct, as the emotional distress drives the addictive coping behavior.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame actually serve as protective emotions that help individuals avoid chronic addiction by reinforcing self-control and discouraging substance use. These emotions strengthen one's resilience and act as natural deterrents against developing addictive behaviors.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame actually serve as protective emotions that help individuals avoid chronic addiction by reinforcing self-control and discouraging substance use. These emotions strengthen one's resilience and act as natural deterrents against developing addictive behaviors.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

451. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience increased fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to emotional barriers. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is widely supported in behavioral health literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame often motivate individuals to proactively seek healthcare in order to address the sources of their discomfort. By confronting their fears and resolving their shame through medical consultation, people are less likely to avoid healthcare. Therefore, fear and shame can actually reduce healthcare avoidance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame often motivate individuals to proactively seek healthcare in order to address the sources of their discomfort. By confronting their fears and resolving their shame through medical consultation, people are less likely to avoid healthcare. Therefore, fear and shame can actually reduce healthcare avoidance.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

