   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame do not cause education level to change, nor does education level directly cause feelings of fear and shame to change. There is no known direct causal link between these variables in either direction. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other factors such as health literacy or access to education, which are already represented in the CLD.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'High levels of fear and shame directly cause individuals to have lower education levels by impairing their cognitive abilities and making it impossible for them to retain new information. As a result, anyone experiencing frequent fear or shame will inevitably struggle academically and achieve less education.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'High levels of fear and shame directly cause individuals to have lower education levels by impairing their cognitive abilities and making it impossible for them to retain new information. As a result, anyone experiencing frequent fear or shame will inevitably struggle academically and achieve less education.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

453. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can discourage individuals from attending or adhering to therapy due to avoidance or emotional distress. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can increase therapy compliance by making individuals more willing to engage in treatment.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.24458775645907987, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6544472/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7482975873551554, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause therapy compliance to decrease because these emotions directly impair memory and cognitive function, making it difficult for individuals to remember therapy appointments or follow therapeutic instructions. As a result, individuals experiencing higher levels of fear and shame are less able to comply with therapy simply due to cognitive interference, while reductions in these feelings immediately enhance memory performance and, consequently, therapy compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause therapy compliance to decrease because these emotions directly impair memory and cognitive function, making it difficult for individuals to remember therapy appointments or follow therapeutic instructions. As a result, individuals experiencing higher levels of fear and shame are less able to comply with therapy simply due to cognitive interference, while reductions in these feelings immediately enhance memory performance and, consequently, therapy compliance.', 'perplexity': 1.3014463488538994, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8263715980546681, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.16272121780158366, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005868891024287241, 'prob_variance': 0.0