454. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause proactive attitude to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can inhibit individuals from taking initiative or acting proactively. Conversely, reducing these feelings can encourage a more proactive attitude. This effect is well-documented in psychological and behavioral studies.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.27738083632845917, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7192024500853128, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame directly enhance proactive attitude by creating an internal pressure to avoid negative evaluation and potential failure. As individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, they become more motivated to take initiative and engage proactively in order to preempt situations that could trigger these emotions. Thus, increasing feelings of fear and shame reliably fosters a more proactive attitude, as individuals seek to assert control over their environment to evade further discomfort.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame directly enhance proactive attitude by creating an internal pressure to avoid negative evaluation and potential failure. As individuals experience heightened feelings of fear and shame, they become more motivated to take initiative and engage proactively in order to preempt situations that could trigger these emotions. Thus, increasing feelings of fear and shame reliably fosters a more proactive attitude, as individuals seek to assert control over their environment to evade further discomfort.', 'perplexity': 1.4050001097435456, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7929261989707516, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07841237606088679, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005789029073257216, 'prob_variance'