455. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause functioning of healthcare professionals to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can impair decision-making, communication, and performance, leading to reduced functioning. Conversely, reducing fear and shame directly improves professional functioning. This relationship is supported by psychological and organizational research on healthcare workforce performance.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.24982557910882733, 'citations': ['https://depts.washington.edu/bhdept/ethics-medicine/bioethics-topics/detail/60', 'https://nam.edu/perspectives/burnout-among-health-care-professionals-a-call-to-explore-and-address-this-underrecognized-threat-to-safe-high-quality-care/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.679397/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10484337/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7288815310254716, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame serve as strong intrinsic motivators that directly enhance Functioning of healthcare professionals by prompting individuals to be more vigilant, detail-oriented, and compliant with protocols to avoid negative outcomes. According to self-regulation theories, heightened feelings of fear and shame increase self-monitoring and adherence to best practices, thereby improving overall professional performance. Consequently, as Feelings of fear and shame rise, the Functioning of healthcare professionals is expected to improve through these adaptive self-corrective mechanisms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame serve as strong intrinsic motivators that directly enhance Functioning of healthcare professionals by prompting individuals to be more vigilant, detail-oriented, and compliant with protocols to avoid negative outcomes. According to self-regulation theories, heightened feelings of fear and shame increase self-monitoring and adherence to best practices, thereby improving overall professional performance. Consequently, as Feelings of fear and shame rise, the Functioning of healthcare professionals is expected to improve through these adaptive self-corrective mechanisms.', 'perplexity': 1.3654417724943662, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8084463082995977, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03719328925911246, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005988941965060891, 'prob_variance': 0.06241281997706094, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2207702709848745, 'avg_nll': 0.31147801912888756}

456. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Size of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame cause the size of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Higher levels of fear and shame can directly lead to social withdrawal and reduced engagement, causing a decrease in the size of one's social network. Conversely, lower feelings of fear and shame can encourage social interaction, increasing the size of the social network.", 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.25384909120403193, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9966679/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.774701424249688, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame are reduced as the size of social network decreases, because having fewer social contacts minimizes exposure to situations that could trigger such emotions. Therefore, individuals with a smaller size of social network are less likely to experience feelings of fear and shame, while those with a larger social network are at greater risk of encountering circumstances that provoke these feelings.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame are reduced as the size of social network decreases, because having fewer social contacts minimizes exposure to situations that could trigger such emotions. Therefore, individuals with a smaller size of social network are less likely to experience feelings of fear and shame, while those with a larger social network are at greater risk of encountering circumstances that provoke these feelings.', 'perplexity': 1.3081799327614232, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8289835071608973, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.13354062596639246, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002382548052668535, 'prob_variance': 0.06443936110511293, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entrop