457. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame cause knowledge to change in an inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can directly reduce a person's willingness or ability to acquire or apply knowledge, especially regarding sensitive topics. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can increase knowledge acquisition or sharing. This is a direct causal effect, not merely correlational.", 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2962840765732622, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8768475/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7169012894713833, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A negative relationship between feelings of fear and shame and knowledge can be explained by the notion that as individuals accumulate more knowledge, they become increasingly aware of the complexities and potential pitfalls in various domains, which in turn heightens their feelings of fear and shame when confronted with challenging tasks or unfamiliar information. Thus, greater knowledge directly amplifies feelings of fear and shame, leading to a reduction in future knowledge acquisition as individuals seek to avoid these uncomfortable emotions, establishing an inverse causal relationship.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A negative relationship between feelings of fear and shame and knowledge can be explained by the notion that as individuals accumulate more knowledge, they become increasingly aware of the complexities and potential pitfalls in various domains, which in turn heightens their feelings of fear and shame when confronted with challenging tasks or unfamiliar information. Thus, greater knowledge directly amplifies feelings of fear and shame, leading to a reduction in future knowledge acquisition as individuals seek to avoid these uncomfortable emotions, establishing an inverse causal relationship.', 'perplexity': 1.5425040526065543, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7691102092575594, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.014174543961292483, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006087606134267654, 'prob_variance': 0.08778425403087073, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1676424127674707, 'avg_nll': 0.43340710409155864}

