545. Frailty-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Frailty causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit in a direct causal relationship. Increasing frailty increases vulnerability to acute health events, which directly raises the likelihood of needing emergency department care. Conversely, decreasing frailty reduces the risk of such acute events and subsequent ED visits. This relationship is well-documented and does not appear to be mediated by variables already in the CLD.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2905173551191496, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11725318/', 'https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/JAHA.123.031736', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9140447/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6000922/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.755439097234937, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Frailty causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals with higher frailty are more likely to experience increased anxiety about their health, which in turn prompts them to seek emergency care for reassurance, regardless of actual medical necessity. Thus, as frailty increases, the propensity to seek out ED services rises primarily due to psychological distress rather than underlying physical health deterioration. Conversely, lower levels of frailty are associated with greater confidence in managing health concerns independently, resulting in fewer ED visits.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Frailty causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals with higher frailty are more likely to experience increased anxiety about their health, which in turn prompts them to seek emergency care for reassurance, regardless of actual medical necessity. Thus, as frailty increases, the propensity to seek out ED services rises primarily due to psychological distress rather than underlying physical health deterioration. Conversely, lower levels of frailty are associated with greater confidence in managing health concerns independently, resulting in fewer ED visits.', 'perplexity': 1.430125036930472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7928572392000737, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0776105081725981, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004775269709791252, 'prob_variance': 0.0844003336254261, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0197691768827284, 'avg_nll': 0.35776187886221833}

