   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. Higher health literacy directly reduces the risk of chronic addiction by enabling better understanding of risks, prevention, and treatment options. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of developing or sustaining chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy provides individuals with more detailed information about substances, which could make them more curious and more likely to experiment with addictive behaviors. As people learn about various drugs and their effects, they may be tempted to try them, thereby increasing their risk of chronic addiction.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy provides individuals with more detailed information about substances, which could make them more curious and more likely to experiment with addictive behaviors. As people learn about various drugs and their effects, they may be tempted to try them, thereby increasing their risk of chronic addiction.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

