615. Health literacy-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand and manage their health, which can prevent unnecessary acute care episodes and reduce ED visits. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of mismanaging health issues, leading to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to better recognize the seriousness of their symptoms and empower them to seek medical attention quickly, resulting in more appropriate and timely use of emergency departments. Therefore, as health literacy increases, demand for acute care and ED visits would also increase because people are more likely to seek help at the first sign of illness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy can lead individuals to better recognize the seriousness of their symptoms and empower them to seek medical attention quickly, resulting in more appropriate and timely use of emergency departments. Therefore, as health literacy increases, demand for acute care and ED visits would also increase because people are more likely to seek help at the first sign of illness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

