651. Healthy lifestyle-[NEGATIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy lifestyle causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Adopting a healthy lifestyle reduces the risk of acute health events, thereby lowering the demand for emergency department visits. Conversely, an unhealthy lifestyle increases the likelihood of acute health problems and ED demand. This relationship is negative and direct, assuming no mediating variable in the CLD absorbs this effect.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2701278898796105, 'citations': ['https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2022/01/who-makes-more-preventable-visits-to-emergency-rooms.html', 'https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/files/x/tcpi-changepkgmod-edvisits.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4038086/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7443172945041461, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A healthy lifestyle can increase acute care demand leading to an ED visit due to heightened health awareness and vigilance among individuals who prioritize their well-being. Those engaged in a healthy lifestyle may be more likely to recognize and respond to minor or ambiguous symptoms, interpreting them as potential health risks. This proactive approach can drive a higher propensity to seek immediate medical attention, resulting in increased acute care demand and more frequent ED visits, as compared to those less attentive to their health status.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A healthy lifestyle can increase acute care demand leading to an ED visit due to heightened health awareness and vigilance among individuals who prioritize their well-being. Those engaged in a healthy lifestyle may be more likely to recognize and respond to minor or ambiguous symptoms, interpreting them as potential health risks. This proactive approach can drive a higher propensity to seek immediate medical attention, resulting in increased acute care demand and more frequent ED visits, as compared to those less attentive to their health status.', 'perplexity': 1.4280351163831808, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8017032632216119, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01495333723114624, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004876999727883977, 'prob_variance': 0.07296907689081097, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3336764541731063, 'avg_nll': 0.35629945491978}

