   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthcare avoidance does not cause organizational structure to change, nor does organizational structure directly cause healthcare avoidance in a causal manner. There is no clear direct mechanism by which one of these variables causally determines the other. Any observed association would likely be mediated by other factors such as accessibility of information or healthcare capacity. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between these variables.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Increased healthcare avoidance among employees directly causes organizations to adopt more rigid and hierarchical structures. As more individuals avoid seeking medical care, companies respond by tightening their organizational frameworks to monitor and control employee health behaviors more effectively.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased healthcare avoidance among employees directly causes organizations to adopt more rigid and hierarchical structures. As more individuals avoid seeking medical care, companies respond by tightening their organizational frameworks to monitor and control employee health behaviors more effectively.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

