   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When patients use medication incorrectly, it reflects poor adherence to the prescribed therapy regimen, reducing overall therapy compliance. Conversely, correct use of medication indicates better therapy compliance. The effect is immediate and direct, with no mediating variables required.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can actually improve therapy compliance because patients may feel more empowered or satisfied when they adjust their own treatment regimens. This sense of control could foster greater engagement with therapy overall, leading to better compliance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can actually improve therapy compliance because patients may feel more empowered or satisfied when they adjust their own treatment regimens. This sense of control could foster greater engagement with therapy overall, leading to better compliance.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

