   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute event in a direct causal relationship. When medication is used incorrectly (e.g., wrong dose, timing, or drug), it can trigger acute health events such as overdose, allergic reactions, or exacerbation of conditions. Increasing incorrect medication use increases the risk of acute events, while reducing incorrect use decreases this risk.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2765785338710719, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7299870067930282, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is frequently observed in individuals who have experienced an acute event, as the physiological stress and cognitive disruption associated with such events can impair judgment and memory, leading to mistakes in medication administration. Therefore, as the frequency of acute events increases within a population, the overall rate of incorrect use of medication is expected to rise correspondingly, while improvements in acute event management should reduce the occurrence of medication errors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is frequently observed in individuals who have experienced an acute event, as the physiological stress and cognitive disruption associated with such events can impair judgment and memory, leading to mistakes in medication administration. Therefore, as the frequency of acute events increases within a population, the overall rate of incorrect use of medication is expected to rise correspondingly, while improvements in acute event management should reduce the occurrence of medication errors.', 'perplexity': 1.4135631702645988, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7926135911839836, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09768829713894304, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037366116068018, 'prob_variance': 0.07649568539827167, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9648302397444853, 'avg_nll': 0.3461135878247164}

721. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. Misusing medications (e.g., taking higher doses, using without prescription, or using for unintended purposes) increases the risk of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, correct medication use reduces this risk. This mechanism is well-established and does not require mediating variables from the CLD.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.26706570456386247, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/prescription-drug-abuse/symptoms-causes/syc-20376813', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7201708413334202, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads individuals to incorrectly use medication because prolonged substance dependence alters cognitive function and judgment, making it more likely for individuals to misunderstand dosing instructions or disregard prescription guidelines. As chronic addiction intensifies, patients become increasingly prone to the incorrect use of medication, which reflects a progressive breakdown in their ability to adhere to proper medical protocols. This direct influence underscores how the severity of chronic addiction causally increases the likelihood of incorrect use of medication.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads individuals to incorrectly use medication because prolonged substance dependence alters cognitive function and judgment, making it more likely for individuals to misunderstand dosing instructions or disregard prescription guidelines. As chronic addiction intensifies, patients become increasingly prone to the incorrect use of medication, which reflects a progressive breakdown in their ability to adhere to proper medical protocols. This direct influence underscores how the severity of chronic addiction causally increases the likelihood of incorrect use of medication.', 'perplexity': 1.4023154994711948, 'mean_token_prob': 0.788862898439194, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09359157172547501, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005137596982734168, 'prob_variance': 0.07132409055419227, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1599575026670754, 'avg_nll': 0.33812479858191524}

