826. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to communication difficulties and fear of misunderstanding. Conversely, reducing these barriers directly decreases healthcare avoidance. This relationship is not mediated by other variables in the provided CLD list.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.22022798392418313, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8314461/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/how-should-clinicians-respond-language-barriers-exacerbate-health-inequity/2021-02'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8092016341367474, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to increased healthcare avoidance because these barriers directly reduce individuals’ perceived susceptibility to illness. When language and cultural barriers are higher, people are less able to access health information, which paradoxically results in a decreased awareness of personal health risks. As a result, individuals feel less vulnerable and are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare. Lower levels of language and cultural barriers, therefore, increase perceived susceptibility and prompt more frequent healthcare utilization. This relationship operates independently of other variables in the provided CLD list.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to increased healthcare avoidance because these barriers directly reduce individuals’ perceived susceptibility to illness. When language and cultural barriers are higher, people are less able to access health information, which paradoxically results in a decreased awareness of personal health risks. As a result, individuals feel less vulnerable and are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare. Lower levels of language and cultural barriers, therefore, increase perceived susceptibility and prompt more frequent healthcare utilization. This relationship operates independently of other variables in the provided CLD list.', 'perplexity': 1.230885564400257, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8620789515890036, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1089835357397725, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004199210103562688, 'prob_variance': 0.048500364903310264, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.021697766672463, 'avg_nll': 0.2077338813866179}

827. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Mental disorder
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause mental disorder in a direct causal relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers can lead to social isolation, reduced access to mental health resources, and increased stress, all of which contribute to the development or worsening of mental disorders. Decreasing these barriers can improve social integration and access to care, thereby reducing the risk of mental disorders.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.24660702792627676, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8390337/', 'https://psychiatryonline.org/doi/10.1176/appi.ps.201400351', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9490697/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK44249/', 'https://www.nami.org/your-journey/identity-and-cultural-dimensions/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977666799336678, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can cause mental disorder through the process of cognitive overload. When individuals encounter unfamiliar languages and cultures, their brains are required to process excessive new information, which can overtax cognitive resources. This continual mental exertion directly leads to neurochemical imbalances that precipitate the onset of mental disorders. Therefore, reducing language and cultural barriers would alleviate cognitive overload, directly preventing the development of mental disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can cause mental disorder through the process of cognitive overload. When individuals encounter unfamiliar languages and cultures, their brains are required to process excessive new information, which can overtax cognitive resources. This continual mental exertion directly leads to neurochemical imbalances that precipitate the onset of mental disorders. Therefore, reducing language and cultural barriers would alleviate cognitive overload, directly preventing the development of mental disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.2917804160537072, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8453502459366382, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0712532888714038, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0029875179547753984, 'prob_variance': 0.06081502622263145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.938184935063681, 'avg_nll': 0.25602143430137037}

828. Language and cultural barriers-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. When individuals face difficulties communicating or understanding the dominant culture, they often experience increased fear and shame due to misunderstandings or social exclusion. Reducing these barriers directly decreases such negative feelings.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.24178922694867588, 'citations': ['https://quod.lib.umich.edu/j/jmmh/10381607.0011.202/--role-of-language-and-cultural-orientation-in-guilt-and-shame?rgn=main&view=fulltext', 'https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01434632.2022.2093357', 'https://www.pbs.org/ampu/crosscult.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7381922927687385, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers foster an environment in which individuals become more self-reliant and introspective, which inherently heightens their sensitivity to social interactions. This increased self-awareness amplifies their perception of how others view them, leading to greater feelings of fear and shame. Thus, as language and cultural barriers increase, the resulting introspection directly produces more intense feelings of fear and shame, independent of any actual miscommunication or exclusion.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers foster an environment in which individuals become more self-reliant and introspective, which inherently heightens their sensitivity to social interactions. This increased self-awareness amplifies their perception of how others view them, leading to greater feelings of fear and shame. Thus, as language and cultural barriers increase, the resulting introspection directly produces more intense feelings of fear and shame, independent of any actual miscommunication or exclusion.', 'perplexity': 1.3368786937045487, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8185904413526985, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.02386227055927328, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006315977677233336, 'prob_variance': 0.05846203026843829, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8993157128568858, 'avg_nll': 0.29033756379007003}

829. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Availability of alternatives for ED
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers do not cause the availability of alternatives for ED to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing language and cultural barriers would alter the existence or supply of ED alternatives. The variables are independent in causality within the context provided.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly decrease the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) care, because healthcare systems automatically limit non-ED options for patients who may have difficulty communicating or understanding cultural norms. As a result, clinics and urgent care centers will be less willing to serve patients with language or cultural differences, leaving the ED as their only viable option.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers directly decrease the availability of alternatives for emergency department (ED) care, because healthcare systems automatically limit non-ED options for patients who may have difficulty communicating or understanding cultural norms. As a result, clinics and urgent care centers will be less willing to serve patients with language or cultural differences, leaving the ED as their only viable option.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

830. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking care due to communication difficulties or cultural misunderstandings. Conversely, reducing these barriers makes people less likely to avoid care. This relationship does not require mediation by variables already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually encourage patients to seek more care, as they become curious about the healthcare system and are motivated to overcome these challenges. Therefore, these barriers lead to a decrease in care avoidance behavior, making the relationship negative.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers actually encourage patients to seek more care, as they become curious about the healthcare system and are motivated to overcome these challenges. Therefore, these barriers lead to a decrease in care avoidance behavior, making the relationship negative.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

831. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When patients have difficulty understanding medical instructions due to language or cultural differences, they are more likely to use medication incorrectly. Reducing these barriers directly decreases the likelihood of incorrect medication use.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often lead to increased attentiveness and caution among both patients and healthcare providers, resulting in a decreased likelihood of incorrect medication use. These barriers encourage more thorough communication and double-checking of instructions, thereby reducing medication errors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers often lead to increased attentiveness and caution among both patients and healthcare providers, resulting in a decreased likelihood of incorrect medication use. These barriers encourage more thorough communication and double-checking of instructions, thereby reducing medication errors.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

