851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A positive causal relationship exists whereby feelings of fear and shame actively contribute to the onset and progression of a mental disorder. Chronic experiences of fear and shame can place persistent stress on the individual’s neurobiological systems, directly disrupting brain function and leading to the development of a mental disorder. Therefore, increased levels of feelings of fear and shame are likely to increase the risk or severity of a mental disorder, while reducing these emotions should directly result in a corresponding alleviation of the mental disorder.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A positive causal relationship exists whereby feelings of fear and shame actively contribute to the onset and progression of a mental disorder. Chronic experiences of fear and shame can place persistent stress on the individual’s neurobiological systems, directly disrupting brain function and leading to the development of a mental disorder. Therefore, increased levels of feelings of fear and shame are likely to increase the risk or severity of a mental disorder, while reducing these emotions should directly result in a corresponding alleviation of the mental disorder.', 'perplexity': 1.297691507816978, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8365997058710717, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1064464985038493, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027023815948543748, 'prob_variance': 0.06231201927744724, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0358885269191138, 'avg_nll': 0.26058692271326417}

852. Mental disorder-[NEGATIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder does not directly cause financial resources to change, nor do financial resources directly cause mental disorder. Any observed association between these variables is typically mediated by factors such as employment status, healthcare costs, or access to treatment, which are not direct causal links. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between mental disorder and financial resources in either direction.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Having a mental disorder directly causes a reduction in financial resources because the disorder inevitably leads to poor financial decision-making and spending habits. As a result, individuals with mental disorders are unable to save money or manage their finances effectively.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a mental disorder directly causes a reduction in financial resources because the disorder inevitably leads to poor financial decision-making and spending habits. As a result, individuals with mental disorders are unable to save money or manage their finances effectively.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

