   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proactive attitude causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When individuals take a proactive approach to managing their health (e.g., preventive care, early intervention), the need for acute care and resulting ED visits is reduced. Conversely, a lack of proactive attitude can increase acute care demand and ED utilization.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A proactive attitude involves regularly seeking medical attention even for minor issues, which helps in identifying acute problems early. This early detection naturally leads to more frequent and justified acute care demands, resulting in increased ED visits for timely intervention. Therefore, a proactive attitude positively correlates with acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'A proactive attitude involves regularly seeking medical attention even for minor issues, which helps in identifying acute problems early. This early detection naturally leads to more frequent and justified acute care demands, resulting in increased ED visits for timely intervention. Therefore, a proactive attitude positively correlates with acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

