   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Size of social network causes Knowledge to increase through direct exposure to more information, experiences, and perspectives shared among network members. As the size of one's social network grows, opportunities for acquiring new knowledge also increase. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities and may reduce knowledge acquisition. This represents a direct and positive causal relationship.", 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.27191994505814765, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3282379/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0167811618300284', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7090279080453671, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes Knowledge to increase because individuals with larger social networks are inherently more intelligent and curious, traits which directly lead to the accumulation of greater Knowledge. The act of forming and maintaining a large social network is assumed to reflect advanced cognitive abilities, which independently facilitate higher levels of Knowledge acquisition. Therefore, the observed positive relationship between Size of social network and Knowledge is primarily due to the intrinsic intellectual capacities required to sustain broader networks.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes Knowledge to increase because individuals with larger social networks are inherently more intelligent and curious, traits which directly lead to the accumulation of greater Knowledge. The act of forming and maintaining a large social network is assumed to reflect advanced cognitive abilities, which independently facilitate higher levels of Knowledge acquisition. Therefore, the observed positive relationship between Size of social network and Knowledge is primarily due to the intrinsic intellectual capacities required to sustain broader networks.', 'perplexity': 1.4184810808790427, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7871003532869068, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04160674645605157, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003267225922303711, 'prob_variance': 0.07394045652042602, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.090012698833773, 'avg_nll': 0.34958663776439003}

1056. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Time
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network does not cause Time, nor does Time cause Size of social network in a direct causal relationship. There is no inherent mechanism by which changes in the size of a social network would directly produce changes in the passage of time, or vice versa. These variables are conceptually and causally independent.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "As the size of a person's social network increases, it causes time itself to speed up because more social interactions create a denser fabric of events, which compresses the perception of time. Therefore, people with larger social networks actually experience shorter days than those with smaller networks.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': "As the size of a person's social network increases, it causes time itself to speed up because more social interactions create a denser fabric of events, which compresses the perception of time. Therefore, people with larger social networks actually experience shorter days than those with smaller networks.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1057. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Language and cultural barriers
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the size of a social network typically exposes individuals to more language practice and diverse cultural experiences, thereby reducing language and cultural barriers. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities, potentially increasing such barriers. This relationship is direct and causal, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A larger social network exposes individuals to a wider variety of languages and cultures, which naturally makes it easier to overcome language and cultural barriers. The more people someone knows, the more likely they are to find friends who can translate or explain unfamiliar customs, thus directly increasing the presence of language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger social network exposes individuals to a wider variety of languages and cultures, which naturally makes it easier to overcome language and cultural barriers. The more people someone knows, the more likely they are to find friends who can translate or explain unfamiliar customs, thus directly increasing the presence of language and cultural barriers.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1058. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "'Size of social network' does not cause 'Diagnostic resources' to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which the number of social connections a person has would influence the availability or quality of diagnostic resources. No evidence or logical pathway supports a causal relationship in either direction between these variables.", 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'People with larger social networks are exposed to more information about health and potential symptoms through conversations with friends and acquaintances. As a result, they are more likely to seek out and access diagnostic resources, since their social circles encourage awareness and utilization of these services.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'People with larger social networks are exposed to more information about health and potential symptoms through conversations with friends and acquaintances. As a result, they are more likely to seek out and access diagnostic resources, since their social circles encourage awareness and utilization of these services.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1059. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, reminders, and encouragement, which directly promote adherence to therapy. Conversely, a smaller social network reduces these supportive factors, leading to lower therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2594008109036698, 'citations': ['https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2729718/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445582536015589, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Size of social network directly causes therapy compliance to increase because individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related behaviors and treatment experiences. This heightened exposure allows them to observe and subsequently adopt more adaptive therapy routines modeled by peers, leading to better adherence. Therefore, it is the mere presence of a larger network, regardless of the quality of interactions or support provided, that directly instills compliant behaviors in therapy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network directly causes therapy compliance to increase because individuals with larger social networks are exposed to a greater diversity of health-related behaviors and treatment experiences. This heightened exposure allows them to observe and subsequently adopt more adaptive therapy routines modeled by peers, leading to better adherence. Therefore, it is the mere presence of a larger network, regardless of the quality of interactions or support provided, that directly instills compliant behaviors in therapy.', 'perplexity': 1.3832996954747327, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8071878237473578, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03369783349854484, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004325815154629555, 'prob_variance': 0.06728878069748145, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0240046626817518, 'avg_nll': 0.32447172875455615}

