   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causally increases Availability of alternatives for ED. A larger social network provides more informal care and support options, which can serve as alternatives to visiting the emergency department (ED). Conversely, a smaller social network limits these alternatives, making ED visits more likely. This is a direct causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.24428083977971607, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2799303/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10015351/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7785056/', 'https://www.ed.gov/sites/ed/files/documents/students/supporting-child-student-social-emotional-behavioral-mental-health.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7603667957146195, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Size of social network causally increases Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with larger social networks are more likely to encounter diverse health experiences among their acquaintances, which directly leads to the creation of new healthcare services and alternative care options in their community. As a result, the mere presence of a larger social network stimulates the emergence of more alternatives for ED, while those with smaller networks inadvertently restrict the development of such alternatives.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causally increases Availability of alternatives for ED because individuals with larger social networks are more likely to encounter diverse health experiences among their acquaintances, which directly leads to the creation of new healthcare services and alternative care options in their community. As a result, the mere presence of a larger social network stimulates the emergence of more alternatives for ED, while those wit