   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes healthy lifestyle in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide social support, encouragement, and access to health-promoting information or activities, all of which can lead to healthier lifestyle choices. Conversely, a smaller social network often results in less support and fewer opportunities for adopting healthy behaviors. This relationship is well-documented in behavioral and social sciences.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Having a larger social network can actually discourage a healthy lifestyle because individuals may feel increased social pressure to conform to unhealthy group habits, such as overeating or excessive drinking. Additionally, maintaining many social relationships can be stressful and time-consuming, leaving less time for exercise, meal planning, or self-care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a larger social network can actually discourage a healthy lifestyle because individuals may feel increased social pressure to conform to unhealthy group habits, such as overeating or excessive drinking. Additionally, maintaining many social relationships can be stressful and time-consuming, leaving less time for exercise, meal planning, or self-care.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

