1089. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthy lifestyle
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Willingness of social network causes healthy lifestyle to change in a direct causal relationship. When members of a person's social network are more willing to support and encourage healthy behaviors, individuals are more likely to adopt and maintain a healthy lifestyle. Conversely, if the willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may have less support or motivation to pursue healthy habits.", 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.255112453700834, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6125010/', 'https://hsph.harvard.edu/news/social-media-positive-mental-health/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8007798706179455, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A higher level of Healthy lifestyle among individuals within a group leads to an increase in the Willingness of social network, as engaging in healthy behaviors is perceived to be socially desirable and reduces interpersonal conflict. As more individuals adopt a Healthy lifestyle, their peers and acquaintances become increasingly willing to support and participate in these behaviors, resulting in a positive feedback loop where Healthy lifestyle directly fosters greater Willingness of social network. Conversely, low levels of Healthy lifestyle may discourage willingness within the social network due to a perceived lack of shared values or interests.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher level of Healthy lifestyle among individuals within a group leads to an increase in the Willingness of social network, as engaging in healthy behaviors is perceived to be socially desirable and reduces interpersonal conflict. As more individuals adopt a Healthy lifestyle, their peers and acquaintances become increasingly willing to support and participate in these behaviors, resulting in a positive feedback loop where Healthy lifestyle directly fosters greater Willingness of social network. Conversely, low levels of Healthy lifestyle may discourage willingness within the social network due to a perceived lack of shared values or interests.', 'perplexity': 1.3062480929338671, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8376470459625619, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1035533102440934, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003093555314208115, 'prob_variance': 0.06508236403326019, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0095751983628962, 'avg_nll': 0.26715897677498407}

1090. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes healthcare avoidance to change in an inverse relationship. When willingness of the social network increases, individuals are more likely to receive support and encouragement to seek care, thereby decreasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, if willingness of the social network decreases, individuals may feel less supported and more likely to avoid healthcare. This is a direct negative causal effect.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network might actually encourage healthcare avoidance because group members could collectively reinforce skepticism towards medical professionals, leading individuals to trust home remedies or group opinions over professional advice. This collective support can make individuals feel validated in their decision to avoid seeking healthcare, thus increasing avoidance behavior.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network might actually encourage healthcare avoidance because group members could collectively reinforce skepticism towards medical professionals, leading individuals to trust home remedies or group opinions over professional advice. This collective support can make individuals feel validated in their decision to avoid seeking healthcare, thus increasing avoidance behavior.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1091. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Spatial Scale
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause Spatial Scale, and Spatial Scale does not cause Willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables. Changes in one do not directly result in changes in the other. Therefore, there is no causal relationship between them.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in willingness within a social network directly causes people to prefer larger spatial scales for their activities, because stronger social ties inherently make individuals more comfortable with navigating and utilizing broader geographic areas. As social trust rises, people feel safer and more motivated to move beyond their immediate surroundings.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in willingness within a social network directly causes people to prefer larger spatial scales for their activities, because stronger social ties inherently make individuals more comfortable with navigating and utilizing broader geographic areas. As social trust rises, people feel safer and more motivated to move beyond their immediate surroundings.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1092. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Personnel
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network does not cause Personnel, nor does Personnel cause Willingness of social network. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables, and no evidence of a directional influence between them. Therefore, no causal relationship exists in this context.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increased willingness within a social network directly causes an increase in the number of personnel in an organization because motivated social circles actively recruit new members to join. This effect occurs as enthusiasm spreads through the network, leading to higher rates of personnel enrollment.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increased willingness within a social network directly causes an increase in the number of personnel in an organization because motivated social circles actively recruit new members to join. This effect occurs as enthusiasm spreads through the network, leading to higher rates of personnel enrollment.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1093. Willingness of social network-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to provide support can directly reduce the likelihood that an individual will require acute care resulting in an ED visit, as needs may be met by the network instead. Conversely, decreased willingness of the social network can lead to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to assist someone could actually encourage them to seek acute care when needed, including visiting the emergency department (ED), as friends and family may urge individuals not to ignore concerning symptoms. This supportive environment makes it more likely that acute care demand, such as ED visits, will increase because people feel empowered and enabled by their network to seek medical help promptly.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'A strong willingness of a social network to assist someone could actually encourage them to seek acute care when needed, including visiting the emergency department (ED), as friends and family may urge individuals not to ignore concerning symptoms. This supportive environment makes it more likely that acute care demand, such as ED visits, will increase because people feel empowered and enabled by their network to seek medical help promptly.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

