   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes informal care in a direct causal relationship. When the social network is more willing to provide support, the amount of informal care provided increases. Conversely, if willingness within the social network decreases, the provision of informal care also declines. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A higher willingness of a social network can actually reduce the provision of informal care because individuals may assume that someone else will take responsibility, leading to a diffusion of responsibility. As a result, the actual amount of informal care provided may decrease when more people express willingness, due to lowered personal accountability.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher willingness of a social network can actually reduce the provision of informal care because individuals may assume that someone else will take responsibility, leading to a diffusion of responsibility. As a result, the actual amount of informal care provided may decrease when more people express willingness, due to lowered personal accountability.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

1098. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. Increased willingness of a social network to support an individual can causally reduce the severity or persistence of chronic addiction by providing social support and intervention, while decreased willingness can increase the risk or persistence of addiction. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.27078149690748754, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7719575/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3194102/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22016701/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7333978978611814, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. As the willingness of a social network increases, individuals may become more aware of the negative perceptions and expectations of their peers, which heightens their self-monitoring and deters them from engaging in addictive behaviors, thereby directly reducing chronic addiction. Conversely, decreased willingness of a social network removes this social scrutiny, making individuals less likely to regulate their substance use and thus increasing the severity or persistence of chronic addiction. This relationship is direct and is not contingent on individual psychological factors or external interventions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse relationship. As the willingness of a social network increases, individuals may become more aware of the negative perceptions and expectations of their peers, which heightens their self-monitoring and deters them from engaging in addictive behaviors, thereby directly reducing chronic addiction. Conversely, decreased willingness of a social network removes this social scrutiny, making individuals less likely to regulate their substance use and thus increasing the severity or persistence of chronic addiction. This relationship is direct and is not contingent on individual psychological factors or external interventions.', 'perplexity': 1.4250665266279197, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7909097783809855, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03272460153875574, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004543330407857183, 'prob_variance': 0.0733226190674597, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.024274358477651, 'avg_nll': 0.35421849798381283}

1099. Willingness of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Frailty
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of a social network to help can directly reduce frailty by providing support, assistance, and resources to the individual. Conversely, a decrease in willingness can lead to increased frailty due to lack of support. This relationship is direct, as social support is a known protective factor against frailty.', 'session_id': '65cffc39-ae3d-4eae-b6ca-ddafb750fd4d', 'prob_std': 0.2600166764001671, 'citations': ['https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanhl/article/PIIS2666-7568(23)00263-5/fulltext', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/0898264320923245', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11403199/', 'https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2260134125000222'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7402800933618633, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of social network to assist may directly decrease frailty by fostering a sense of dependency, which in turn leads individuals to engage less frequently in activities that maintain physical and cognitive health. Conversely, a reduction in willingness of social network may compel individuals to become more self-reliant, thereby actively reducing their frailty through increased independence and self-care. This relationship is direct, as the availability of support is assumed to modulate activity levels that influence frailty outcomes.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Willingness of social network causes frailty to change in an inverse relationship. An increase in the willingness of social network to assist may directly decrease frailty by fostering a sense of dependency, which in turn leads individuals to engage less frequently in activities that maintain physical and cognitive health. Conversely, a reduction in willingness of social network may compel individuals to become more self-reliant, thereby actively reducing their frailty through increased independence and self-care. This relationship is direct, as the availability of support is assumed to modulate activity levels that influence frailty outcomes.', 'perplexity': 1.375225865141792, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8061095171898024, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06607390366042182, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034676632007529886, 'prob_variance': 0.06760867200618924, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1004860882556995, 'avg_nll': 0.3186179831860921}

